\pard\qc\b Intelligent Airport Gate Assignment Using Machine Learning and Multi-Objective Optimization\b0\par
\pard\qc R. Balaji\par
\pard\qc Final Year Project Prototype\par
\pard\sa180\par
\pard\b Abstract.\b0 Airport gate assignment affects turnaround coordination, passenger movement, and airside efficiency. This document reports an implementation-driven prototype for the Airport Gate Assignment Problem built from a synthetic airport operations pipeline, a delay-prediction model selection layer, and a constrained optimization engine. The system generates realistic flight and gate records, trains multiple regression models to estimate delay, and uses the selected model inside a genetic-algorithm-based search to optimize gate allocation under gate-type, gate-size, and time-overlap constraints. In the exported experiment with 30 flights, 8 gates, and random seed 42, the best predictive model was Random Forest with RMSE 8.855 and MAE 7.993. Compared with the random baseline assignment used in evaluation, the optimized assignment reduced CO2 proxy by 5.628 percent and reduced average predicted delay by 0.829 percent, while increasing average passenger walking distance by 9.760 percent. The study demonstrates a transparent academic prototype for combining prediction and optimization in airport operations research.\par
\pard\sa120\b Keywords.\b0 Airport Gate Assignment Problem, AGAP, machine learning, genetic algorithm, Streamlit, multi-objective optimization.\par
\pard\sa180\par
\pard\b 1. Introduction\b0\par
\pard Airport gate assignment is a constrained decision problem in which flights must be matched to limited gate resources while respecting aircraft compatibility, terminal service requirements, and schedule conflicts. Poor assignments can increase taxiing effort, passenger walking burden, and disruption propagation. This project implements a compact research prototype that connects prediction and optimization in one pipeline. Instead of optimizing purely static cost terms, the system estimates delay behavior from generated operational features and uses those predictions as part of the gate-assignment fitness function.\par
\pard\sa120\b 2. Problem Statement\b0\par
\pard The implemented system solves a constrained AGAP variant in which each flight must be assigned to exactly one gate. A feasible assignment must satisfy four rules: gate-size compatibility, gate-type compatibility, non-overlap between temporally conflicting flights at the same gate, and complete assignment coverage. The optimization objective combines passenger walking cost, taxiing cost, CO2 proxy cost, and predicted delay penalty, with infeasible chromosomes penalized during search.\par
\pard\sa120\b 3. Repository-Driven System Design\b0\par
\pard The implementation is modular. The file src/data.py generates synthetic gates and flights. The file src/model.py builds features, trains candidate regressors, and extracts feature importance. The file src/optimize.py defines compatibility checks, the fitness function, a genetic-algorithm-style search process, and a first-fit fallback. The file src/evaluate.py computes assignment metrics. The file src/pipeline.py orchestrates one complete experiment and exports CSV outputs. The application is exposed through app.py and run_pipeline.py.\par
\pard\sa120\b 4. Methodology\b0\par
\pard\b 4.1 Synthetic Data Generation.\b0 Flight attributes include flight identifier, arrival and departure time, aircraft type, flight type, passenger demand, historical delay, taxi distances, fuel burn rate, and an initial assigned gate. Gate attributes include gate identifier, gate type, gate size, runway distance, and terminal-center distance. The generator creates clustered arrivals and realistic turnaround durations so that gate conflicts arise naturally.\par
\pard\b 4.2 Delay Prediction Layer.\b0 The learning target is historical_delay_minutes. The implemented feature set uses aircraft type, flight type, arrival hour, expected passengers, taxi distance feature, and fuel burn rate. The candidate models are Linear Regression, Random Forest Regressor, and XGBoost when available. RMSE and MAE are used for model selection.\par
\pard\b 4.3 Optimization Layer.\b0 The optimizer represents one chromosome as a full gate assignment across flights. The implemented objective weights are 0.35 for walking cost, 0.20 for taxi cost, 0.40 for CO2 proxy cost, and 0.05 for predicted delay cost. The search loop uses initialization, fitness evaluation, elite retention, one-point crossover, mutation, and early stopping. If the final best chromosome is infeasible, the implementation falls back to a first-fit assignment.\par
\pard\sa120\b 5. Experimental Setup\b0\par
\pard The current exported results correspond to the repository default configuration: 30 flights, 8 gates, and random seed 42. The evaluation baseline in src/pipeline.py is a random gate assignment, while the optimizer internally uses a feasible first-fit assignment as its initial reference chromosome.\par
