\pard\sa120\b 7. Discussion\b0\par
\pard The implementation succeeds as an integrated research pipeline because data generation, model selection, optimization, evaluation, dashboard presentation, and CSV export all run through a single experiment workflow. The results also show a realistic multi-objective trade-off rather than an unrealistic improvement in every metric. The reported gains should therefore be interpreted as controlled proof-of-concept evidence, not deployment-ready airport performance estimates.\par
\pard\sa120\b 8. Limitations\b0\par
\pard 1. The dataset is synthetic and does not capture full airport operational variability.\par
\pard 2. The environmental metric is a comparative CO2 proxy, not a validated emissions inventory.\par
\pard 3. The evaluation baseline is random, so stronger baselines could narrow the apparent gains.\par
\pard 4. The experiments are based on single exported runs rather than repeated trials with confidence intervals.\par
\pard 5. No exact optimization benchmark is included for comparison.\par
\pard\sa120\b 9. Conclusion and Future Work\b0\par
\pard This implementation demonstrates that a delay-aware gate assignment workflow can be built as a coherent academic prototype using synthetic data, regression-based delay prediction, and multi-objective evolutionary search. In the current exported run, the system improved CO2 proxy and predicted delay relative to the evaluation baseline, while exposing a measurable walking-distance trade-off. Future work should focus on real airport datasets, repeated multi-seed experiments, stronger baselines, exact-solver comparisons, and richer objective terms such as passenger connections, fairness across gates, and disruption recovery.\par
